(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("service-mesh",{"service-mesh/kubernetes-networking-under-the-hood":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes promises that every pod can reach every other pod by IP address, without NAT. Something has to make that true. Knowing what that something is turns a mysterious timeout into a short list of places to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The four rules of the Kubernetes network model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a CNI plugin does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a Service, kube-proxy and endpoints turn a name into a pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How DNS works inside a cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingress and the Gateway API for traffic coming from outside\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model\">The model\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Every pod gets its own IP address.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pods can reach all other pods without NAT.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Agents on a node can reach all pods on that node.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A pod sees itself with the same IP address others see.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>The cluster does not implement this itself. A \u003cstrong>CNI plugin\u003c/strong> (Container Network Interface), such as Calico, Cilium or Flannel, runs when a pod starts. It creates a virtual network device for the pod, assigns an address from the node&#39;s pod range and sets up the routes or tunnels that let other nodes reach it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-a-service-name-to-a-pod\">From a Service name to a pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pods come and go, so their addresses change. A \u003cstrong>Service\u003c/strong> gives a stable virtual IP (the ClusterIP) and a name. The pieces:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Service has a \u003cstrong>selector\u003c/strong>. Pods whose labels match become its \u003cstrong>endpoints\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>kube-proxy\u003c/strong> on each node programs rules (iptables or IPVS) that rewrite traffic sent to the ClusterIP so it goes to one of the endpoint pod IPs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CoreDNS\u003c/strong> answers \u003ccode>web.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code> with the ClusterIP.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get svc,endpoints web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc,endpoints web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME          TYPE        CLUSTER-IP     PORT(S)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">service/web   ClusterIP   10.96.14.20    80/TCP\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            ENDPOINTS                         AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">endpoints/web   10.244.1.7:8080,10.244.2.4:8080   3d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the \u003cstrong>ENDPOINTS\u003c/strong> column is empty, the selector matches no ready pods. That is the most common cause of &quot;the Service does not work&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dns-in-the-cluster\">DNS in the cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pods get \u003ccode>/etc/resolv.conf\u003c/code> pointing at CoreDNS and a search path such as \u003ccode>shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local\u003c/code>. So \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> resolves inside the \u003ccode>shop\u003c/code> namespace, and \u003ccode>web.other\u003c/code> reaches another namespace. A setting called \u003ccode>ndots:5\u003c/code> makes short names try the search path first, which can slow lookups for external names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coming-in-from-outside\">Coming in from outside\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>NodePort\u003c/strong> opens a port on every node. A \u003cstrong>LoadBalancer\u003c/strong> Service asks the cloud (or MetalLB on bare metal) for an external IP.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ingress\u003c/strong> routes HTTP by host and path to Services, run by an ingress controller such as NGINX.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>Gateway API\u003c/strong> is the newer, more expressive model: a \u003ccode>Gateway\u003c/code> for the listener, \u003ccode>HTTPRoute\u003c/code> for rules, with roles split between platform and application teams.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Clients get &quot;connection refused&quot; through a Service called web. Fix it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># restart the pods and the Service\nkubectl rollout restart deployment/web -n shop\nkubectl delete svc web -n shop &amp;&amp; kubectl apply -f svc.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl get endpoints web -n shop\nNAME   ENDPOINTS   AGE\nweb    &lt;none&gt;      12s\n# still no endpoints, and now there was an outage while the Service was deleted\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Restarting changes nothing about labels. The Service selects app=web but the pods are labelled app=webapp, so no pod matches. Deleting the Service also removed its ClusterIP for a while.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl get svc web -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'\nkubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels\n# fix the selector or the label so they match\nkubectl get endpoints web -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Compare the selector with the pod labels first. A Service with empty endpoints is a label problem until proven otherwise.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-dns-slowed-everything-down\">Incident: DNS slowed everything down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Calls to an external API took five seconds, but only from pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-7d -- cat /etc/resolv.conf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-7d -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/resolv.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nameserver 10.96.0.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">options ndots:5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The name \u003ccode>api.partner.com\u003c/code> has two dots, fewer than five, so the resolver tried each search suffix first and waited for failures. Ending the name with a dot (\u003ccode>api.partner.com.\u003c/code>) or lowering \u003ccode>ndots\u003c/code> cut the time to milliseconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Blaming the app when the Service has no endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that the selector and labels must match exactly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming DNS is always fast inside a cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using NodePort in production without a load balancer in front\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model","text":"The model"},{"id":"from-a-service-name-to-a-pod","text":"From a Service name to a pod"},{"id":"dns-in-the-cluster","text":"DNS in the cluster"},{"id":"coming-in-from-outside","text":"Coming in from outside"},{"id":"incident-dns-slowed-everything-down","text":"Incident: DNS slowed everything down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Cluster networking","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/networking/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Service","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/"},{"t":"Gateway API","u":"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"}],"text":"Kubernetes promises that every pod can reach every other pod by IP address, without NAT. Something has to make that true. Knowing what that something is turns a mysterious timeout into a short list of places to look. What you will learn The four rules of the Kubernetes network model What a CNI plugin does How a Service, kube-proxy and endpoints turn a name into a pod How DNS works inside a cluster Ingress and the Gateway API for traffic coming from outside The model Every pod gets its own IP address. Pods can reach all other pods without NAT. Agents on a node can reach all pods on that node. A pod sees itself with the same IP address others see. The cluster does not implement this itself. A CNI plugin (Container Network Interface), such as Calico, Cilium or Flannel, runs when a pod starts. It creates a virtual network device for the pod, assigns an address from the node&#39;s pod range and sets up the routes or tunnels that let other nodes reach it. From a Service name to a pod Pods come and go, so their addresses change. A Service gives a stable virtual IP (the ClusterIP) and a name. The pieces: The Service has a selector . Pods whose labels match become its endpoints . kube-proxy on each node programs rules (iptables or IPVS) that rewrite traffic sent to the ClusterIP so it goes to one of the endpoint pod IPs. CoreDNS answers web.shop.svc.cluster.local with the ClusterIP. $ kubectl get svc,endpoints web -n shop NAME TYPE CLUSTER-IP PORT(S) service/web ClusterIP 10.96.14.20 80/TCP NAME ENDPOINTS AGE endpoints/web 10.244.1.7:8080,10.244.2.4:8080 3d If the ENDPOINTS column is empty, the selector matches no ready pods. That is the most common cause of \"the Service does not work\". DNS in the cluster Pods get /etc/resolv.conf pointing at CoreDNS and a search path such as shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local . So web resolves inside the shop namespace, and web.other reaches another namespace. A setting called ndots:5 makes short names try the search path first, which can slow lookups for external names. Coming in from outside A NodePort opens a port on every node. A LoadBalancer Service asks the cloud (or MetalLB on bare metal) for an external IP. Ingress routes HTTP by host and path to Services, run by an ingress controller such as NGINX. The Gateway API is the newer, more expressive model: a Gateway for the listener, HTTPRoute for rules, with roles split between platform and application teams. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Clients get \"connection refused\" through a Service called web. Fix it. The tempting way # restart the pods and the Service kubectl rollout restart deployment/web -n shop kubectl delete svc web -n shop && kubectl apply -f svc.yaml See what goes wrong $ kubectl get endpoints web -n shop NAME ENDPOINTS AGE web \u003cnone> 12s # still no endpoints, and now there was an outage while the Service was deleted Why it fails. Restarting changes nothing about labels. The Service selects app=web but the pods are labelled app=webapp, so no pod matches. Deleting the Service also removed its ClusterIP for a while. See the way that works The way that works kubectl get svc web -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}' kubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels # fix the selector or the label so they match kubectl get endpoints web -n shop Compare the selector with the pod labels first. A Service with empty endpoints is a label problem until proven otherwise. Incident: DNS slowed everything down Incident Calls to an external API took five seconds, but only from pods. $ kubectl exec web-7d -- cat /etc/resolv.conf search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local nameserver 10.96.0.10 options ndots:5 The name api.partner.com has two dots, fewer than five, so the resolver tried each search suffix first and waited for failures. Ending the name with a dot ( api.partner.com. ) or lowering ndots cut the time to milliseconds. Common mistakes Blaming the app when the Service has no endpoints Forgetting that"},"service-mesh/cni-network-policy-and-ebpf":{"html":"\u003cp>By default every pod can talk to every other pod. That is convenient for a small cluster and risky for a shared one. Network policies and the right CNI let you say who may talk to whom.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How CNIs differ: overlay, routed, eBPF\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a NetworkPolicy does and the default-deny pattern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why a policy does nothing without support from the CNI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What eBPF changes in Cilium\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to test a policy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-a-cni\">Choosing a CNI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Flannel\u003c/strong>: simple overlay networking, no policy support.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Calico\u003c/strong>: routed or overlay networking with strong policy and BGP options.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cilium\u003c/strong>: built on \u003cstrong>eBPF\u003c/strong>, with policy, observability and optional replacement of kube-proxy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) give pods addresses from the cloud network.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>overlay\u003c/strong> wraps pod traffic in tunnels (VXLAN) between nodes, which is easy but adds a small overhead. \u003cstrong>Routed\u003c/strong> designs advertise pod ranges to the network, which is faster and easier to debug with ordinary tools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-networkpolicy-says\">What a NetworkPolicy says\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists the traffic \u003cstrong>allowed\u003c/strong> to or from them. Policies are additive: if no policy selects a pod, everything is allowed; once any policy selects it for a direction, only what is listed in some policy is allowed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]\n---\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: web-from-ingress\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: web\n  policyTypes: [Ingress]\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - namespaceSelector:\n        matchLabels:\n          name: ingress-nginx\n    ports:\n    - port: 8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Egress\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web-from-ingress\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaceSelector:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ingress-nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first policy selects every pod and allows nothing: \u003cstrong>default deny\u003c/strong>. The second allows only the ingress controller to reach \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> on 8080. Remember to allow DNS (port 53 to CoreDNS) when you deny egress, or lookups break.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"policy-needs-enforcement\">Policy needs enforcement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy is only a rule. The \u003cstrong>CNI\u003c/strong> has to enforce it. Applying one on a cluster running Flannel is accepted without error and does nothing, which is a dangerous silent failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-ebpf-changes\">What eBPF changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>eBPF\u003c/strong> lets small, checked programs run inside the Linux kernel. Cilium uses it to:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enforce policy by identity (labels), not by IP address\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replace long iptables chains with fast hash lookups, which matters with thousands of Services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Show who talks to whom, with tools like Hubble\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cilium status --brief\nkubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- curl -s -m 2 http://db.shop:5432\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cilium status --brief\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OK\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> -n shop deploy/web -- curl -s -m 2 http://db.shop:5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (28) Connection timed out after 2001 milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A timeout where you expect a refusal often means a policy is silently dropping the packets.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Lock down the shop namespace so only the ingress controller can reach the web pods.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># apply a default-deny policy and nothing else, in production, on a Friday\nkubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- nslookup db.shop\n;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\n# every pod lost DNS and all traffic stopped, including health checks\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Default deny with Egress included blocks DNS and every other call. Nothing was allowed first, so the whole namespace went dark at once.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># 1. observe real traffic first (Hubble or flow logs)\n# 2. write allow rules: ingress to web, web to db, all pods to kube-dns on 53\n# 3. test in a staging namespace\n# 4. apply default-deny last\nkubectl apply -f allow-dns.yaml -f allow-web.yaml -n shop\nkubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Add the allows before the deny, test them, and keep a rollback command ready.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-policy-that-did-nothing\">Incident: The policy that did nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security review found the database reachable from any namespace, despite a NetworkPolicy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get cm -n kube-system | grep -E &quot;flannel|calico|cilium&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cm -n kube-system | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;flannel|calico|cilium&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kube-flannel-cfg   2   200d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster used plain Flannel, which does not enforce NetworkPolicy. The policy objects existed, but nothing applied them. The team moved to a CNI with enforcement and added a test that tries a blocked connection in CI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming a policy is enforced without testing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Denying egress without allowing DNS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using IP addresses in policy instead of labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying default-deny without first learning the real traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"choosing-a-cni","text":"Choosing a CNI"},{"id":"what-a-networkpolicy-says","text":"What a NetworkPolicy says"},{"id":"policy-needs-enforcement","text":"Policy needs enforcement"},{"id":"what-ebpf-changes","text":"What eBPF changes"},{"id":"incident-the-policy-that-did-nothing","text":"Incident: The policy that did nothing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Network Policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"},{"t":"Cilium documentation","u":"https://docs.cilium.io/"},{"t":"Project Calico documentation","u":"https://docs.tigera.io/calico/latest/about/"}],"text":"By default every pod can talk to every other pod. That is convenient for a small cluster and risky for a shared one. Network policies and the right CNI let you say who may talk to whom. What you will learn How CNIs differ: overlay, routed, eBPF What a NetworkPolicy does and the default-deny pattern Why a policy does nothing without support from the CNI What eBPF changes in Cilium How to test a policy Choosing a CNI Flannel : simple overlay networking, no policy support. Calico : routed or overlay networking with strong policy and BGP options. Cilium : built on eBPF , with policy, observability and optional replacement of kube-proxy. Cloud CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) give pods addresses from the cloud network. An overlay wraps pod traffic in tunnels (VXLAN) between nodes, which is easy but adds a small overhead. Routed designs advertise pod ranges to the network, which is faster and easier to debug with ordinary tools. What a NetworkPolicy says A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists the traffic allowed to or from them. Policies are additive: if no policy selects a pod, everything is allowed; once any policy selects it for a direction, only what is listed in some policy is allowed. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ Ingress , Egress ] --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: web-from-ingress namespace: shop spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: web policyTypes: [ Ingress ] ingress: - from: - namespaceSelector: matchLabels: name: ingress-nginx ports: - port: 8080 The first policy selects every pod and allows nothing: default deny . The second allows only the ingress controller to reach web on 8080. Remember to allow DNS (port 53 to CoreDNS) when you deny egress, or lookups break. Policy needs enforcement A NetworkPolicy is only a rule. The CNI has to enforce it. Applying one on a cluster running Flannel is accepted without error and does nothing, which is a dangerous silent failure. What eBPF changes eBPF lets small, checked programs run inside the Linux kernel. Cilium uses it to: Enforce policy by identity (labels), not by IP address Replace long iptables chains with fast hash lookups, which matters with thousands of Services Show who talks to whom, with tools like Hubble $ cilium status --brief OK $ kubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- curl -s -m 2 http://db.shop:5432 curl: (28) Connection timed out after 2001 milliseconds A timeout where you expect a refusal often means a policy is silently dropping the packets. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Lock down the shop namespace so only the ingress controller can reach the web pods. The tempting way # apply a default-deny policy and nothing else, in production, on a Friday kubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop See what goes wrong $ kubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- nslookup db.shop ;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached # every pod lost DNS and all traffic stopped, including health checks Why it fails. Default deny with Egress included blocks DNS and every other call. Nothing was allowed first, so the whole namespace went dark at once. See the way that works The way that works # 1. observe real traffic first (Hubble or flow logs) # 2. write allow rules: ingress to web, web to db, all pods to kube-dns on 53 # 3. test in a staging namespace # 4. apply default-deny last kubectl apply -f allow-dns.yaml -f allow-web.yaml -n shop kubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop Add the allows before the deny, test them, and keep a rollback command ready. Incident: The policy that did nothing Incident A security review found the database reachable from any namespace, despite a NetworkPolicy. $ kubectl get cm -n kube-system | grep -E \"flannel|calico|cilium\" kube-flannel-cfg 2 200d The cluster used plain Flannel, which does not enforce NetworkPolicy. The policy objects existed, but nothing applied them. The team moved to a CNI with enforc"},"service-mesh/what-a-service-mesh-is-and-how-it-works":{"html":"\u003cp>When a company has hundreds of services, every one needs the same things: encrypted calls, retries, timeouts, metrics, access rules. A service mesh moves those jobs out of the application code and into the platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The problem a mesh solves, and what it costs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data plane and control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sidecar proxies, and the newer ambient mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The main meshes: Istio, Linkerd, Cilium\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to decide whether you need one\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-problem\">The problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Without a mesh, each team writes its own retry logic, its own TLS setup and its own metrics. They differ by language, and a fix means redeploying every service. With a mesh, a proxy beside each service handles the network side the same way for everyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-planes\">Two planes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>data plane\u003c/strong> is the proxies that carry the real traffic. In Istio the proxy is \u003cstrong>Envoy\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>control plane\u003c/strong> (Istio&#39;s \u003ccode>istiod\u003c/code>) is the brain. It watches Kubernetes and your mesh configuration, turns them into proxy configuration and pushes it out. It also issues the certificates used for mTLS.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop\nkubectl get pod web-7d9f-x2 -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7d9f-x2   2/2     Running   0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod web-7d9f-x2 -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[*].name}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web istio-proxy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>2/2\u003c/code> ready means the pod runs the app and a proxy sidecar. Traffic in and out of the app is redirected through the proxy automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sidecar-and-ambient\">Sidecar and ambient\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sidecar\u003c/strong>: a proxy in every pod. It is simple to reason about and gives per-pod control, but it costs memory and CPU in every pod, and pods must be restarted to pick up proxy upgrades.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ambient\u003c/strong> (Istio): a shared node-level component handles encryption and basic policy (L4), and an optional per-namespace proxy handles HTTP features (L7). It lowers the per-pod cost.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Injection works through a label on the namespace (\u003ccode>istio-injection=enabled\u003c/code>). Pods created \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> the label do not get a sidecar until they restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-main-meshes\">The main meshes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mesh\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Style\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Known for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Istio\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Envoy, rich features\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Powerful traffic control, larger to operate\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Linkerd\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Small Rust proxy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simplicity, low overhead\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cilium\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>eBPF, sidecar-free option\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Network integration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-not-to-use-one\">When not to use one\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A mesh adds moving parts, a new failure mode and a learning curve. If you have a handful of services, or need only TLS, a library or an ingress may be enough. Adopt a mesh for a clear need: mutual TLS everywhere, uniform telemetry, or fine traffic control.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Turn on the mesh for the shop namespace and get mTLS for all services.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># label the namespace and expect everything to change\nkubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl get pods -n shop\nNAME          READY   STATUS\nweb-7d9f-x2   1/1     Running\n# still one container: no sidecar, no mTLS\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Injection happens when a pod is created. Pods that already existed were never changed. The label only affects new pods.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled\nkubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop\nkubectl get pods -n shop\nistioctl proxy-status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Restart the workloads so the webhook can add the sidecar, then check that every pod reads 2/2 and every proxy shows SYNCED.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-mesh-upgrade-that-took-the-checkout-down\">Incident: A mesh upgrade that took the checkout down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a control plane upgrade, some services returned 503 errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl proxy-status\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            CDS      LDS      EDS      RDS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout-1.shop STALE    SYNCED   SYNCED   SYNCED\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Some proxies were stuck on old configuration because the control plane restarted before they reconnected. Rolling the control plane one replica at a time, and waiting for proxy-status to show SYNCED before each next step, avoided the problem on the next upgrade.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adopting a mesh with no concrete problem to solve\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting existing pods to get sidecars without a restart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the extra CPU and memory the proxies need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading the control plane with no staged rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-problem","text":"The problem"},{"id":"two-planes","text":"Two planes"},{"id":"sidecar-and-ambient","text":"Sidecar and ambient"},{"id":"the-main-meshes","text":"The main meshes"},{"id":"when-not-to-use-one","text":"When not to use one"},{"id":"incident-a-mesh-upgrade-that-took-the-checkout-down","text":"Incident: A mesh upgrade that took the checkout down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio: Architecture","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/deployment/architecture/"},{"t":"Linkerd documentation","u":"https://linkerd.io/2/overview/"},{"t":"Envoy documentation","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/"}],"text":"When a company has hundreds of services, every one needs the same things: encrypted calls, retries, timeouts, metrics, access rules. A service mesh moves those jobs out of the application code and into the platform. What you will learn The problem a mesh solves, and what it costs Data plane and control plane Sidecar proxies, and the newer ambient mode The main meshes: Istio, Linkerd, Cilium How to decide whether you need one The problem Without a mesh, each team writes its own retry logic, its own TLS setup and its own metrics. They differ by language, and a fix means redeploying every service. With a mesh, a proxy beside each service handles the network side the same way for everyone. Two planes The data plane is the proxies that carry the real traffic. In Istio the proxy is Envoy . The control plane (Istio&#39;s istiod ) is the brain. It watches Kubernetes and your mesh configuration, turns them into proxy configuration and pushes it out. It also issues the certificates used for mTLS. $ kubectl get pods -n shop NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS web-7d9f-x2 2/2 Running 0 $ kubectl get pod web-7d9f-x2 -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.containers[*].name}&#x27; web istio-proxy 2/2 ready means the pod runs the app and a proxy sidecar. Traffic in and out of the app is redirected through the proxy automatically. Sidecar and ambient Sidecar : a proxy in every pod. It is simple to reason about and gives per-pod control, but it costs memory and CPU in every pod, and pods must be restarted to pick up proxy upgrades. Ambient (Istio): a shared node-level component handles encryption and basic policy (L4), and an optional per-namespace proxy handles HTTP features (L7). It lowers the per-pod cost. Injection works through a label on the namespace ( istio-injection=enabled ). Pods created before the label do not get a sidecar until they restart. The main meshes Mesh Style Known for Istio Envoy, rich features Powerful traffic control, larger to operate Linkerd Small Rust proxy Simplicity, low overhead Cilium eBPF, sidecar-free option Network integration When not to use one A mesh adds moving parts, a new failure mode and a learning curve. If you have a handful of services, or need only TLS, a library or an ingress may be enough. Adopt a mesh for a clear need: mutual TLS everywhere, uniform telemetry, or fine traffic control. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Turn on the mesh for the shop namespace and get mTLS for all services. The tempting way # label the namespace and expect everything to change kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled See what goes wrong $ kubectl get pods -n shop NAME READY STATUS web-7d9f-x2 1/1 Running # still one container: no sidecar, no mTLS Why it fails. Injection happens when a pod is created. Pods that already existed were never changed. The label only affects new pods. See the way that works The way that works kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled kubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop kubectl get pods -n shop istioctl proxy-status Restart the workloads so the webhook can add the sidecar, then check that every pod reads 2/2 and every proxy shows SYNCED. Incident: A mesh upgrade that took the checkout down Incident After a control plane upgrade, some services returned 503 errors. $ istioctl proxy-status NAME CDS LDS EDS RDS checkout-1.shop STALE SYNCED SYNCED SYNCED Some proxies were stuck on old configuration because the control plane restarted before they reconnected. Rolling the control plane one replica at a time, and waiting for proxy-status to show SYNCED before each next step, avoided the problem on the next upgrade. Common mistakes Adopting a mesh with no concrete problem to solve Expecting existing pods to get sidecars without a restart Ignoring the extra CPU and memory the proxies need Upgrading the control plane with no staged rollout"},"service-mesh/mtls-and-traffic-management":{"html":"\u003cp>Once the proxies are in place, the mesh gives you two big powers: every call can be encrypted and authenticated, and you can steer traffic with configuration instead of code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mutual TLS, identity and the PeerAuthentication modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Authorisation policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VirtualService and DestinationRule for routing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary releases with weights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking, and how they can hurt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mutual-tls\">Mutual TLS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With ordinary TLS only the server proves who it is. In \u003cstrong>mutual TLS (mTLS)\u003c/strong> both sides do. The mesh gives each workload an identity (based on its service account), a short-lived certificate, and rotates it automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PeerAuthentication\u003c/code> sets the mode:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>PERMISSIVE\u003c/strong>: accepts both mTLS and plain text. Good while you migrate.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>STRICT\u003c/strong>: only mTLS. Plain text callers are rejected.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: PeerAuthentication\nmetadata:\n  name: default\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  mtls:\n    mode: STRICT\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">security.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PeerAuthentication\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mtls:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">STRICT\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003ccode>AuthorizationPolicy\u003c/code> then says who may call what, for example only the \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> service account may call \u003ccode>GET /orders\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routing-virtualservice-and-destinationrule\">Routing: VirtualService and DestinationRule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>DestinationRule\u003c/strong> defines \u003cstrong>subsets\u003c/strong> of a service, usually by pod label (\u003ccode>version: v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>version: v2\u003c/code>), and policies such as connection limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>VirtualService\u003c/strong> decides where requests go: by weight, by header, by path.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: DestinationRule\nmetadata:\n  name: reviews\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  host: reviews\n  subsets:\n  - name: v1\n    labels:\n      version: v1\n  - name: v2\n    labels:\n      version: v2\n---\napiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: VirtualService\nmetadata:\n  name: reviews\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  hosts:\n  - reviews\n  http:\n  - route:\n    - destination:\n        host: reviews\n        subset: v1\n      weight: 90\n    - destination:\n        host: reviews\n        subset: v2\n      weight: 10\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DestinationRule\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subsets:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualService\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This sends 10 percent of calls to version 2: a \u003cstrong>canary\u003c/strong>. Raise the weight as metrics stay healthy. The weights in one route must add up to 100, and a subset named in a VirtualService must exist in a DestinationRule, or the proxy returns errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl analyze -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl analyze -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: &quot;reviews+v2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-retries-and-circuit-breaking\">Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>timeout\u003c/strong> stops a call from waiting forever.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>retry\u003c/strong> repeats a failed call, which hides short faults but can multiply load.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Circuit breaking\u003c/strong> (connection limits and outlier detection) stops sending traffic to a pod that keeps failing.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>If service A retries three times and calls B, which retries three times and calls C, one slow C produces up to nine calls for each request: a \u003cstrong>retry storm\u003c/strong>. Put retries at one layer, use short per-try timeouts and cap the total.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Send 10 percent of reviews traffic to version 2 as a canary.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># apply only the VirtualService\nkubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ curl -s http://reviews:9080/ -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\n&quot;\n503\n$ istioctl analyze -n shop\nError [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: &quot;reviews+v2&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> A VirtualService can name a subset, but the subset is defined in a DestinationRule. Without it the proxy has no endpoints for &quot;v2&quot; and answers 503 to the requests routed there.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl apply -f reviews-destinationrule.yaml\nkubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml\nistioctl analyze -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Create the DestinationRule first, then the VirtualService, and run analyze before you watch the dashboards.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-retries-made-the-outage-worse\">Incident: Retries made the outage worse\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A slow database caused a full outage of the storefront.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Three layers each retried failed calls three times with no limit. When the database slowed, each user request became dozens of calls, which overloaded it further. Removing retries from the inner layers, adding per-try timeouts, and enabling outlier detection restored stability.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Switching to STRICT before every caller has a sidecar\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Weights that do not add up to 100\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries at several layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a VirtualService needs its subsets to exist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"mutual-tls","text":"Mutual TLS"},{"id":"routing-virtualservice-and-destinationrule","text":"Routing: VirtualService and DestinationRule"},{"id":"timeouts-retries-and-circuit-breaking","text":"Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking"},{"id":"incident-retries-made-the-outage-worse","text":"Incident: Retries made the outage worse"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio: Traffic management","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/traffic-management/"},{"t":"Istio: Security","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"Envoy: Circuit breaking","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/upstream/circuit_breaking"}],"text":"Once the proxies are in place, the mesh gives you two big powers: every call can be encrypted and authenticated, and you can steer traffic with configuration instead of code. What you will learn Mutual TLS, identity and the PeerAuthentication modes Authorisation policy VirtualService and DestinationRule for routing Canary releases with weights Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking, and how they can hurt Mutual TLS With ordinary TLS only the server proves who it is. In mutual TLS (mTLS) both sides do. The mesh gives each workload an identity (based on its service account), a short-lived certificate, and rotates it automatically. PeerAuthentication sets the mode: PERMISSIVE : accepts both mTLS and plain text. Good while you migrate. STRICT : only mTLS. Plain text callers are rejected. apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1 kind: PeerAuthentication metadata: name: default namespace: shop spec: mtls: mode: STRICT An AuthorizationPolicy then says who may call what, for example only the web service account may call GET /orders . Routing: VirtualService and DestinationRule A DestinationRule defines subsets of a service, usually by pod label ( version: v1 , version: v2 ), and policies such as connection limits. A VirtualService decides where requests go: by weight, by header, by path. apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: DestinationRule metadata: name: reviews namespace: shop spec: host: reviews subsets: - name: v1 labels: version: v1 - name: v2 labels: version: v2 --- apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: VirtualService metadata: name: reviews namespace: shop spec: hosts: - reviews http: - route: - destination: host: reviews subset: v1 weight: 90 - destination: host: reviews subset: v2 weight: 10 This sends 10 percent of calls to version 2: a canary . Raise the weight as metrics stay healthy. The weights in one route must add up to 100, and a subset named in a VirtualService must exist in a DestinationRule, or the proxy returns errors. $ istioctl analyze -n shop Error [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: \"reviews+v2\" Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking A timeout stops a call from waiting forever. A retry repeats a failed call, which hides short faults but can multiply load. Circuit breaking (connection limits and outlier detection) stops sending traffic to a pod that keeps failing. If service A retries three times and calls B, which retries three times and calls C, one slow C produces up to nine calls for each request: a retry storm . Put retries at one layer, use short per-try timeouts and cap the total. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Send 10 percent of reviews traffic to version 2 as a canary. The tempting way # apply only the VirtualService kubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml See what goes wrong $ curl -s http://reviews:9080/ -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} \" 503 $ istioctl analyze -n shop Error [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: \"reviews+v2\" Why it fails. A VirtualService can name a subset, but the subset is defined in a DestinationRule. Without it the proxy has no endpoints for \"v2\" and answers 503 to the requests routed there. See the way that works The way that works kubectl apply -f reviews-destinationrule.yaml kubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml istioctl analyze -n shop Create the DestinationRule first, then the VirtualService, and run analyze before you watch the dashboards. Incident: Retries made the outage worse Incident A slow database caused a full outage of the storefront. Three layers each retried failed calls three times with no limit. When the database slowed, each user request became dozens of calls, which overloaded it further. Removing retries from the inner layers, adding per-try timeouts, and enabling outlier detection restored stability. Common mistakes Switching to STRICT before every caller has a sidecar Weights that do not add up to 100 Retries at several layers"},"service-mesh/observing-and-troubleshooting-the-mesh":{"html":"\u003cp>A mesh adds a hop to every call, which makes problems harder to place: is it the app, the proxy or the configuration? The same proxies that add the hop also give you the best visibility you will ever have.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The metrics and traces a mesh produces for free\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking sync with \u003ccode>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>analyze\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading proxy response flags in access logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A step-by-step method for a failing call\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-get-for-free\">What you get for free\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every proxy reports the \u003cstrong>golden signals\u003c/strong> for each service pair: request rate, error rate, latency and saturation. Add distributed tracing headers and you can see a request&#39;s path through services. Dashboards such as Kiali (a topology graph), Grafana and Jaeger show it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"is-the-configuration-there\">Is the configuration there?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before blaming the app, check that the proxy has the configuration you wrote.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl proxy-status\nistioctl analyze -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME             CLUSTER      CDS      LDS      EDS      RDS      ISTIOD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7d.shop      Kubernetes   SYNCED   SYNCED   SYNCED   SYNCED   istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">reviews-9a.shop  Kubernetes   SYNCED   STALE    SYNCED   SYNCED   istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl analyze -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Info [IST0118] (Service shop/web) Port name  (port: 80, targetPort: 8080) doesn't follow the naming convention of Istio port.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>STALE\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>NOT SENT\u003c/strong> means the proxy has not received or accepted the latest configuration.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>analyze\u003c/code> checks your configuration for mistakes. Port names that do not start with a protocol (\u003ccode>http-\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>grpc-\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tcp-\u003c/code>) make the mesh treat the traffic as plain TCP, so HTTP features silently do not apply.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-response-flags\">Reading response flags\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Envoy access logs show a short \u003cstrong>response flag\u003c/strong> that says why a request failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Flag\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Look at\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>UF\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Upstream connection failure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Is the destination pod up? mTLS mode mismatch?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>UC\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Upstream connection terminated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>App closed the connection, timeouts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>NR\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No route configured\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VirtualService host or Gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>UH\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No healthy upstream\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Endpoints empty, readiness probes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>URX\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retry limit exceeded\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backend failing; retries too high\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DC\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Downstream connection terminated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Client gave up\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[2026-11-03T10:12:01.221Z] &quot;GET /orders HTTP/1.1&quot; 503 UH no_healthy_upstream - 0 19 0 - &quot;-&quot; &quot;curl/8&quot; &quot;reviews.shop.svc.cluster.local:9080&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>503 UH\u003c/code> for the reviews service tells you no healthy endpoint exists, so check the selector, readiness and rollout of reviews.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-method-for-a-failing-call\">A method for a failing call\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Which hop?\u003c/strong> Use the trace or the access logs to find the first failing hop.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Does the proxy have current config?\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Is the config valid?\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>istioctl analyze\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What does the flag say?\u003c/strong> Read the response flag in the log.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Does the destination have endpoints?\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>kubectl get endpoints\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Is it a policy?\u003c/strong> Check PeerAuthentication and AuthorizationPolicy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Take the mesh out of the picture.\u003c/strong> Call the pod IP directly to see whether the app is the problem.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Calls from web to reviews suddenly return 503. Find the cause.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># assume the app is broken and redeploy reviews, then scale it up\nkubectl rollout restart deployment/reviews -n shop\nkubectl scale deployment/reviews --replicas=10 -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -1\n&quot;GET /reviews HTTP/1.1&quot; 503 UF upstream_reset_before_response_started{connection_termination}\n# still failing with more pods\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> UF means the connection to the upstream failed. The new PeerAuthentication in reviews is STRICT, but web has no sidecar so it calls in plain text. More replicas cannot fix a protocol mismatch.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl get pods -n shop\nistioctl proxy-status\nkubectl get peerauthentication -A\n# give web a sidecar, or set PERMISSIVE until it has one\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Read the response flag before changing anything. UF with a recent mTLS change is a mismatch between a plain text caller and a STRICT destination.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everything-is-up-nothing-works\">Incident: Everything is up, nothing works\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The checkout page returned errors, but every pod was Running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/code> showed 40 proxies STALE. The control plane had run out of memory and restarted, and it could not push new configuration. Raising its memory limit and adding a second replica fixed it. Without proxy-status the team would have spent hours inspecting healthy pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking at the app first when the proxy configuration is stale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Port names without a protocol prefix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring response flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a single control plane replica\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-you-get-for-free","text":"What you get for free"},{"id":"is-the-configuration-there","text":"Is the configuration there?"},{"id":"reading-response-flags","text":"Reading response flags"},{"id":"a-method-for-a-failing-call","text":"A method for a failing call"},{"id":"incident-everything-is-up-nothing-works","text":"Incident: Everything is up, nothing works"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio: Debugging","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/diagnostic-tools/"},{"t":"Envoy: Access logging response flags","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/configuration/observability/access_log/usage"},{"t":"Google SRE book: Monitoring","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"}],"text":"A mesh adds a hop to every call, which makes problems harder to place: is it the app, the proxy or the configuration? The same proxies that add the hop also give you the best visibility you will ever have. What you will learn The metrics and traces a mesh produces for free Checking sync with istioctl proxy-status and analyze Reading proxy response flags in access logs A step-by-step method for a failing call What you get for free Every proxy reports the golden signals for each service pair: request rate, error rate, latency and saturation. Add distributed tracing headers and you can see a request&#39;s path through services. Dashboards such as Kiali (a topology graph), Grafana and Jaeger show it. Is the configuration there? Before blaming the app, check that the proxy has the configuration you wrote. $ istioctl proxy-status NAME CLUSTER CDS LDS EDS RDS ISTIOD web-7d.shop Kubernetes SYNCED SYNCED SYNCED SYNCED istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp reviews-9a.shop Kubernetes SYNCED STALE SYNCED SYNCED istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp $ istioctl analyze -n shop Info [IST0118] (Service shop/web) Port name (port: 80, targetPort: 8080) doesn't follow the naming convention of Istio port. STALE or NOT SENT means the proxy has not received or accepted the latest configuration. analyze checks your configuration for mistakes. Port names that do not start with a protocol ( http- , grpc- , tcp- ) make the mesh treat the traffic as plain TCP, so HTTP features silently do not apply. Reading response flags Envoy access logs show a short response flag that says why a request failed. Flag Meaning Look at UF Upstream connection failure Is the destination pod up? mTLS mode mismatch? UC Upstream connection terminated App closed the connection, timeouts NR No route configured VirtualService host or Gateway UH No healthy upstream Endpoints empty, readiness probes URX Retry limit exceeded Backend failing; retries too high DC Downstream connection terminated Client gave up $ kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -2 [2026-11-03T10:12:01.221Z] \"GET /orders HTTP/1.1\" 503 UH no_healthy_upstream - 0 19 0 - \"-\" \"curl/8\" \"reviews.shop.svc.cluster.local:9080\" 503 UH for the reviews service tells you no healthy endpoint exists, so check the selector, readiness and rollout of reviews. A method for a failing call Which hop? Use the trace or the access logs to find the first failing hop. Does the proxy have current config? istioctl proxy-status . Is the config valid? istioctl analyze . What does the flag say? Read the response flag in the log. Does the destination have endpoints? kubectl get endpoints . Is it a policy? Check PeerAuthentication and AuthorizationPolicy. Take the mesh out of the picture. Call the pod IP directly to see whether the app is the problem. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Calls from web to reviews suddenly return 503. Find the cause. The tempting way # assume the app is broken and redeploy reviews, then scale it up kubectl rollout restart deployment/reviews -n shop kubectl scale deployment/reviews --replicas=10 -n shop See what goes wrong $ kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -1 \"GET /reviews HTTP/1.1\" 503 UF upstream_reset_before_response_started{connection_termination} # still failing with more pods Why it fails. UF means the connection to the upstream failed. The new PeerAuthentication in reviews is STRICT, but web has no sidecar so it calls in plain text. More replicas cannot fix a protocol mismatch. See the way that works The way that works kubectl get pods -n shop istioctl proxy-status kubectl get peerauthentication -A # give web a sidecar, or set PERMISSIVE until it has one Read the response flag before changing anything. UF with a recent mTLS change is a mismatch between a plain text caller and a STRICT destination. Incident: Everything is up, nothing works Incident The checkout page returned errors, but every pod was Running. istioctl proxy-status showed 40 proxies STALE. The control plane had run out of memory and restarted, and it "}});